- What class should implement the Comparable interface?
- Answer: The class media should implement the Comparable interface cause:
+ All media items(Book, DigitalVideoDisc, CompactDisk) share the same base type Media.
+ The cart contains a list of medias.
+ Sorting doesn't care about the specific media types.
- In those classes, how should you implement the compareTo()method be to reflect the ordering that we 
want?
-Answer: For title then cost, overide compareTo() with this code
@Override
public int compareTo(Media other) {
    int titleCompare = this.getTitle().compareToIgnoreCase(other.getTitle());
    if (titleCompare != 0)
        return titleCompare;
    return Float.compare(this.getCost(), other.getCost());
}
For cost then title:
@Override
public int compareTo(Media other) {
    int costCompare = Float.compare(this.getCost(), other.getCost());
    if (costCompare != 0) {
        return costCompare;
    }
    return this.getTitle().compareToIgnoreCase(other.getTitle());
}

- Can we have two ordering rules of the item (by title then cost and by cost then title) if we use this 
Comparable interface approach?
-Answer: No, Comparable gives a single natural ordering. You can only have one implementation of compareTo() per class.
- Suppose the DVDs has a different ordering rule from the other media types, that is by title, then decreasing 
length, then cost. How would you modify your code to allow this? 
-Answer: We can O=override compareTo() only in DVD class but now two DVD objects sort differently than other media types.
The better option is using Comparator instead of Comparable for all sorting as each media type can have its own comparator
and you can plug in any comparator into the sorting method.
